---
import StandardPageLayout from "../../layouts/StandardPageLayout.astro";
import FinancialStatementKey from "../../components/FinancialStatementKey.astro";
import Glossary from "../../components/Glossary.astro";
import PageToc from "../../components/PageToc.astro";
import "../../styles/standards-poster.css";
import "../../styles/extended.css";
import HistoryTimeline from "../../components/HistoryTimeline.astro";
import history from "../../data/history.json";
import metadata from "../../data/standards/usgaap/asc922.json";
---

<StandardPageLayout
  code={metadata.code}
  standardType={metadata.framework}
  title={metadata.title}
  description={metadata.description}
  issued={metadata.issued}
  latestAmendment={metadata.latestAmendment}
  status={metadata.status}
  summary={metadata.summary}
  bannerNotice={metadata.bannerNotice}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        { id: "s1", label: "Conceptual Framework Linkage" },
        { id: "s2", label: "Scope" },
        { id: "s3", label: "Capitalizable Initial Subscriber Installation (Hookup) Costs" },
        { id: "s4", label: "Hookup Revenue" },
        { id: "s5", label: "The Prematurity Period — Capitalizing Costs During System Development" },
        { id: "s6", label: "Subsequent Operating Costs — Normal Expensing Once Mature" },
        { id: "s7", label: "Worked Example — Initial Hookup Cost Capitalization" },
        { id: "s8", label: "Worked Example — Prematurity Period Cost Deferral" },
        { id: "s9", label: "Disclosure Requirements" },
        { id: "s10", label: "Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates" },
        { id: "s11", label: "No Dedicated IFRS Cable Standard — Discussion" },
        { id: "s12", label: "Statement Mapping" },
        { id: "s13", label: "Related Topics" },
        { id: "s14", label: "Glossary" }
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>
  <div class="wrap">

    <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Matching costs to a system's genuine developmental phase</div><div>A NEWLY constructed cable television system typically operates BELOW its intended long-term capacity while its subscriber base is still GROWING — during this "PREMATURITY PERIOD," certain OPERATING costs may not yet be matched by proportionate revenue simply because the system hasn't reached its planned scale. ASC 922 addresses how to capitalise SUBSCRIBER installation (hookup) costs and, in defined circumstances, certain costs incurred during this developmental prematurity period.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Cable and satellite television system operators</div><div>ASC 922 applies to entities operating CABLE TELEVISION systems (and analogous subscriber-based video distribution systems), addressing costs of initial subscriber hookups and system construction/development.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>Capitalizable Initial Subscriber Installation (Hookup) Costs</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Direct costs of connecting a new subscriber</div><div>The DIRECT costs of INITIALLY connecting a subscriber to the cable system — labor and materials for the drop cable, converter box installation, and related direct costs — are CAPITALISED as part of the SUBSCRIBER installation asset, since they give rise to a lasting economic benefit (the ongoing connection enabling future subscription revenue), rather than being treated as period costs of a one-time transaction.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>Hookup Revenue</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">A nonrefundable upfront fee, deferred under general ASC 606 principles</div><div>Any UPFRONT hookup/installation FEE charged to the new subscriber is evaluated under GENERAL ASC 606 principles — since the installation itself typically does NOT represent a distinct performance obligation providing standalone value apart from the ONGOING subscription service, such fees are generally DEFERRED and recognised as revenue over the expected SUBSCRIBER RELATIONSHIP period, rather than recognised immediately upon connection.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>The Prematurity Period — Capitalizing Costs During System Development</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">A defined window for deferring certain operating costs during system buildout</div><div>During a system's DEFINED "PREMATURITY PERIOD" — the period during which the system is still being CONSTRUCTED and its subscriber base is still being BUILT toward planned, mature-market scale — certain SYSTEM-level costs (e.g. specific operating and depreciation charges attributable to the developing, not-yet-mature portion of the system) may be CAPITALISED rather than expensed as incurred, PROVIDED specific criteria establishing genuine "prematurity" are satisfied and the deferral period is appropriately DEFINED and LIMITED at the outset. This is a NARROW, specific exception to normal expense recognition, reflecting the unique construction/ramp-up economics of a capital-intensive subscriber network business.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Subsequent Operating Costs — Normal Expensing Once Mature</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Standard expense recognition applies once the system matures</div><div>Once a system has passed its DEFINED prematurity period (or for systems that never qualified for prematurity treatment), ONGOING operating costs — programming costs, maintenance, customer service — are expensed as INCURRED under NORMAL GAAP principles, with NO further special deferral.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Worked Example — Initial Hookup Cost Capitalization</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Capitalising a direct connection cost</div>
    <div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: Connecting a new residential subscriber</div><div class="wk-body">A cable operator incurs $180 of direct labor and materials cost to install service for a new residential subscriber, and charges the subscriber a nonrefundable $50 installation fee.</div><div class="wk-answer">The operator CAPITALISES the $180 direct installation cost as a subscriber installation asset, DEPRECIATED over its estimated useful life (i.e. the expected subscriber relationship period). The $50 installation FEE charged to the subscriber is DEFERRED and recognised as revenue over that SAME expected subscriber relationship period, rather than recognised immediately.</div></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Worked Example — Prematurity Period Cost Deferral</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Deferring costs during system buildout</div>
    <div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: A newly constructed system ramping toward planned capacity</div><div class="wk-body">A newly built cable system is designed for 50,000 mature subscribers but has only 8,000 subscribers connected during its defined 18-month prematurity period, incurring certain system-level operating costs during this ramp-up window that meet the specific qualifying criteria for prematurity treatment.</div><div class="wk-answer">During the DEFINED 18-month prematurity period, QUALIFYING system-level costs attributable to the not-yet-mature portion of the system may be CAPITALISED rather than expensed — deferred until the system reaches its planned operating scale, at which point normal expensing resumes for all ongoing operating costs.</div></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Disclosure Requirements</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">What ASC 922 requires</div><div class="check-list"><span>Accounting policy for capitalising subscriber installation costs and the related depreciation period</span><span>Description of any prematurity period costs deferred, and the defined prematurity period</span></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cn span2"><div class="ctitle cn">Where judgement matters most</div>
    <div class="two-col"><div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Whether a system genuinely qualifies for prematurity period cost deferral, and the appropriate length of that period</span></div></div>
    <div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Expected subscriber relationship period used to depreciate installation costs and defer hookup fee revenue</span></div></div></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>No Dedicated IFRS Cable Standard — Discussion</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">General PP&amp;E and revenue principles apply instead</div><div>IFRS has NO dedicated cable television industry standard. A cable operator reporting under IFRS would apply GENERAL PP&amp;E capitalisation principles (IAS 16) to subscriber installation costs, and GENERAL IFRS 15 principles to hookup fee revenue deferral — reaching BROADLY similar practical outcomes to ASC 922's capitalisation and deferral treatment, but WITHOUT a codified, industry-specific "prematurity period" concept of the kind ASC 922 provides.</div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Where every ASC 922 event lands</div>
    <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Event</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
    <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Subscriber installation cost</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span></span><span>Capitalised, depreciated over the expected subscriber relationship</span></div>
    <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Hookup fee revenue</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span> <span class="badge bp">IS</span></span><span>Deferred and recognised over the expected subscriber relationship period</span></div>
    </div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Related Topics</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Cross-reference map</div>
    <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Topic</span><span>Connection</span></div>
    <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>ASC 360</span><span>Property, Plant &amp; Equipment — supplies the general capitalisation and depreciation framework</span></div>
    <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>ASC 606</span><span>Governs deferral of nonrefundable hookup fee revenue over the expected customer relationship</span></div>
    </div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cn span2">
    <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Prematurity period</div><div class="glossary-def">A defined developmental window during which a newly constructed cable system is still building its subscriber base toward planned scale, permitting narrow cost deferral under specific criteria.</div></div>
    <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Hookup cost</div><div class="glossary-def">The direct labor and materials cost of initially connecting a new subscriber to the cable system, capitalised and depreciated over the expected subscriber relationship.</div></div>
    </div>
    </div>

    <FinancialStatementKey />

  </div>
  <section id="history">
    <HistoryTimeline items={history.asc922 || []} />
  </section>
</StandardPageLayout>
